        <article id="learnerbly">
            <h1>From a hypothesis about workplace learning to a product people actually use.</h1>
            <section>
                <h2>The situation</h2>
                <p>Learnerbly came to Make Us Proud as an idea: a platform that would help employees access learning resources without the bureaucracy of traditional L&amp;D. The founders had conviction. What they needed was someone to make it real — to turn the idea into something they could put in front of users, investors and eventually a team.</p>
            </section>
            <section>
                <h2>What I did</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Led the research and discovery phase — [brief description of what that involved, e.g. user interviews, market mapping]</li>
                    <li>Defined the product scope and shaped the MVP — what to build first, what to leave out</li>
                    <li>[Design work — describe your involvement]</li>
                    <li>Worked closely with the founders to make sure the product matched their vision without getting lost in it</li>
                </ul>
            </section>
            <section>
                <h2>The outcome</h2>
                <p>The v1 launched [date/timeframe]. Learnerbly has since [current status — funding, users, team size — worth a quick Google to verify].</p>
            </section>
            <section>
                <h2>What it taught me</h2>
                <p>The hardest part of early-stage product work isn't knowing what to build — it's agreeing on what not to build. A good first version is mostly decisions about what to leave out.</p>
            </section>
        </article>

        <article id="tate-sensorium">
            <h1>An exhibition that asked: what if art wasn't just visual?</h1>
            <section>
                <h2>The situation</h2>
                <p>Tate Britain commissioned an experimental exhibition that would pair four artworks with multisensory experiences — taste, touch, sound, scent — triggered by what visitors were seeing. Nobody had done quite this before, which meant there was no template to follow.</p>
            </section>
